Yes. Athleticism is just one capability stack. You can map a lot of human performance the same way: base capacities → coordination layer → domain skill → strategy → expression.

Here’s a useful cross-domain map:

Stack	Base layer	Coordination layer	Applied layer	High-level expression
Athleticism	strength, speed, endurance, mobility	balance, coordination, reaction	sprinting, jumping, throwing, cutting	sport performance
Cognition	attention, memory, processing speed	abstraction, comparison, inhibition	reasoning, planning, problem solving	judgment
Learning	curiosity, attention, recall	pattern recognition, chunking, transfer	study, practice, feedback	mastery
Creativity	perception, memory, imagination	association, recombination, variation	writing, design, invention	originality
Communication	vocabulary, perception, emotional awareness	framing, timing, tone, compression	speaking, writing, persuasion	shared understanding
Social intelligence	empathy, observation, self-awareness	theory of mind, calibration, rapport	negotiation, influence, cooperation	relationship navigation
Leadership	competence, credibility, emotional regulation	prioritization, delegation, coordination	decisions, direction, culture	collective performance
Strategy	observation, memory, models	prediction, tradeoffs, sequencing	positioning, resource allocation	advantage
Tactical ability	perception, reaction, technical skill	orientation, timing, adaptation	execution under pressure	local victory
Resilience	physical health, emotional regulation	stress tolerance, reframing, recovery	persistence, adaptation	durability
Courage	fear recognition, values	inhibition control, commitment	action despite risk	principled behavior
Discipline	energy, attention	impulse control, habit architecture	consistency, follow-through	reliability
Entrepreneurship	curiosity, risk tolerance, domain knowledge	opportunity recognition, synthesis	building, selling, iterating	value creation
Business	capital, labor, information	processes, incentives, coordination	product, sales, operations	enterprise performance
Engineering	math, physics, materials	modeling, decomposition, systems thinking	design, test, build	reliable systems
Artistry	sensory discrimination, technique	taste, composition, timing	medium-specific execution	expression
Storytelling	language, memory, emotion	character, tension, pacing, causality	scenes, narrative structure	meaning
Wisdom	knowledge, experience	perspective, value weighting, uncertainty	judgment across contexts	good decisions
Agency	energy, autonomy, belief in action	goal formation, prioritization	execution, adaptation	changing reality

A deeper abstraction underneath most of them is:

substrate → capacity → control → coordination → skill → strategy → adaptation → expression

For example:

Strength is a capacity.
Motor control coordinates it.
A punch is a skill.
When to punch is tactics.
Whether to fight at all is strategy.
Learning from the outcome is adaptation.

Same structure for thinking:

memory → reasoning → problem solving → decision making → strategy → judgment

And for language:

words → syntax → rhetoric → conversation → persuasion → shared meaning

The interesting part is that many stacks share the same hidden operators:

perception, memory, prediction, timing, control, coordination, energy management, feedback, adaptation.

Those may be closer to the real “primitives” underneath human capability. Athleticism, leadership, creativity, strategy, and even storytelling are different arrangements of many of the same underlying components.

A useful Pantheon-style compression would be:

Sense → Represent → Orient → Generate → Select → Act → Measure → Adapt

Then each domain simply supplies different sensors, actuators, constraints, and objectives.